# The Tale of the Twin Rivers

In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite banks of a sacred river. The River Spirit blessed them with a curious gift: any offering cast from one shore would triple by the time it reached the other.

The villages took turns making offerings. When the North Village sent grain across, it became threefold, feeding the South Village abundantly. The South Village, grateful and wise, would return half to the North, leaving both better than before. The cycle continued, roles reversing with each moon.

But one harsh winter, fear crept into hearts. The North Village held back, sending only scraps. The South, receiving little, returned even less. Soon both villages starved, the river's magic wasted.

An elder remembered the old ways. "The River Spirit rewards trust," she said. "The multiplication is not just in the crossing—it's in the returning."

Hesitantly, the North sent half their stores. It tripled. The South, moved by such faith in desperate times, returned generously. The North reciprocated. Soon abundance flowed both ways, each village thriving not despite their giving, but because of it.

The villagers learned: magic lies not in what multiplies across the water, but in what flows back—and forward—again and again.
